1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,603
I've been looking at ocean biodiversity,

2
00:00:05,603 --> 00:00:09,600
that's the diversity of species that live in the world's oceans.

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:17,490
About 20 years ago biologists developed the idea of what they called 'biodiversity hotspots'.

4
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:21,888
These are the areas which have the greatest mixture of species,

5
00:00:21,888 --> 00:00:24,720
so one example is Madagascar.

6
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:31,753
These hotspots are significant because they allow us to locate key areas for focusing

7
00:00:31,753 --> 00:00:33,240
efforts at conservation.

8
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:37,860
Biologists can identify hotspots on land, fairly easily,

9
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:43,142
but until recently, very little was known about species distribution

10
00:00:43,142 --> 00:00:44,970
and diversity in the oceans,

11
00:00:44,970 --> 00:00:48,630
and no one even knew if hotspots existed there.

12
00:00:48,630 --> 00:00:56,771
Then a Canadian biologist called Boris Worm did some research in 2005 on data

13
00:00:56,771 --> 00:01:00,260
on ocean species that he got from the fishing industry.

14
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:06,509
Worm located five hotspots for large ocean predators like sharks,

15
00:01:06,509 --> 00:01:08,880
and looked at what they had in common.

16
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:14,736
The main thing he'd expected to find was that they had very high concentrations

17
00:01:14,736 --> 00:01:15,400
of food,

18
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:20,056
but to his surprise that was only true for four of the hotspots -

19
00:01:20,056 --> 00:01:24,190
the remaining hotspot was quite badly off in that regard.

20
00:01:24,190 --> 00:01:29,027
But what he did find was that in all cases, the water at the

21
00:01:29,027 --> 00:01:32,600
surface of the ocean had relatively high temperatures,

22
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:35,452
even when it was cool at greater depths,

23
00:01:35,452 --> 00:01:40,715
so this seemed to be a factor in supporting a diverse range of these

24
00:01:40,715 --> 00:01:41,860
large predators.

25
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:45,373
However, this wasn't enough on its own,

26
00:01:45,373 --> 00:01:50,130
because he also found that the water needed to have enough oxygen in it -

27
00:01:50,130 --> 00:01:55,976
so these two factors seemed necessary to support the high metabolic rate of these

28
00:01:55,976 --> 00:01:56,780
large fish.

29
00:01:56,780 --> 00:02:03,551
A couple of years later, in 2007, a researcher called Lisa Ballance,

30
00:02:03,551 --> 00:02:05,380
who was working in California,

31
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:08,460
also started looking for ocean hotspots,

32
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,070
but not for fish

33
00:02:10,070 --> 00:02:15,190
what she was interested in was marine mammals, things like seals.

34
00:02:15,190 --> 00:02:19,300
And she found three places in the oceans which were hotspots,

35
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:24,066
and what these had in common was that these hotspots were all located at

36
00:02:24,066 --> 00:02:26,600
boundaries between ocean currents,

37
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,699
and this seems to be the sort of place that has lots of the

38
00:02:30,699 --> 00:02:33,230
plankton that some of these species feed on.

39
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:38,932
So now people who want to protect the species that are endangered need to

40
00:02:38,932 --> 00:02:41,020
get as much information as possible.

41
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:46,240
For example, there's an international project called the Census of Marine Life.

42
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:50,940
They've been surveying oceans all over the world, including the Arctic.

43
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:56,827
One thing they found there which stunned other researchers was that there were large

44
00:02:56,827 --> 00:02:59,740
numbers of species which live below the ice

45
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:03,590
sometimes under a layer up to 20 metres thick.

46
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:07,750
Some of these species had never been seen before.

47
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:12,530
They've even found species of octopus living in these conditions.

48
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:16,445
And other scientists working on the same project,

49
00:03:16,445 --> 00:03:20,560
but researching very different habitats on the ocean floor,

50
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:28,006
have found large numbers of species congregating around volcanoes, attracted to them by the warmth

51
00:03:28,006 --> 00:03:29,210
and nutrients there.

52
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:40,756
However, biologists still don't know how serious the threat to their survival is for

53
00:03:40,756 --> 00:03:42,500
each individual species.

54
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:49,230
So a body called the Global Marine Species Assessment is now creating a list

55
00:03:49,230 --> 00:03:51,360
of endangered species on land,

56
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:55,470
so they consider things like the size of the population

57
00:03:55,470 --> 00:03:59,785
how many members of one species there are in a particular place -

58
00:03:59,785 --> 00:04:04,120
and then they look at their distribution in geographical terms,

59
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:07,847
although this is quite difficult when you're looking at fish,

60
00:04:07,847 --> 00:04:09,620
because they're so mobile,

61
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:14,736
and then thirdly they calculate the rate at which the decline of the species

62
00:04:14,736 --> 00:04:15,280
is happening.

63
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:21,500
So far only 1,500 species have been assessed,

64
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:24,490
but they want to increase this figure to 20,000.

65
00:04:24,490 --> 00:04:29,989
For each one they assess, they use the data they collect on that species

66
00:04:29,989 --> 00:04:33,060
to produce a map showing its distribution.

67
00:04:33,060 --> 00:04:40,449
Ultimately they will be able to use these to figure out not only where most species are located

68
00:04:40,449 --> 00:04:43,180
but also where they are most threatened.

69
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:49,860
So finally，what can be done to retain the diversity of species in the world's

70
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:49,910
oceans?

71
00:04:49,910 --> 00:04:56,365
Firstly, we need to set up more reserves in our oceans, places where marine

72
00:04:56,365 --> 00:04:57,790
species are protected.

73
00:04:57,790 --> 00:04:59,072
We have some,

74
00:04:59,072 --> 00:05:00,070
but not enough.

75
00:05:00,070 --> 00:05:04,944
In addition, to preserve species such as leatherback turtles,

76
00:05:04,944 --> 00:05:09,497
which live out in the high seas but have their nesting sites on the

77
00:05:09,497 --> 00:05:10,540
American coast,

78
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:13,919
we need to create corridors for migration,

79
00:05:13,919 --> 00:05:17,820
so they can get from one area to another safely.

80
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:23,102
As well as this, action needs to be taken to lower the levels of

81
00:05:23,102 --> 00:05:27,560
fishing quotas to prevent overfishing of endangered species.

82
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:31,420
And finally, there's the problem of "by-catch".

83
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:36,758
This refers to the catching of unwanted fish by fishing boats - they're returned

84
00:05:36,758 --> 00:05:37,481
to the sea,

85
00:05:37,481 --> 00:05:40,250
but they're often dead or dying.

86
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:45,874
If these commercial fishing boats used equipment which was more selective,

87
00:05:45,874 --> 00:05:49,730
so that only the fish wanted for consumption were caught,

88
00:05:49,730 --> 00:05:52,000
this problem could be overcome.

89
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,408
OK,

90
00:05:53,408 --> 00:05:57,290
So does anyone have any ...
